<Layout
  title={"The Reality Code Engine: One Lossless Form for Native, Verified, Low-Energy Programming"}
  description={"About: The Reality Code Engine: One Lossless Form for Native, Verified, Low-Energy Programming. Public main at commit 1762fb4b (20 November 2026): 5,537 Lean 4 theorem constants (T1 to T63), 2,221/2,236 conformance tests passing (0 fail), 837 Rust unit tests, and first Apple M5 Max reported_j receipts (49.04 mJ/Form check, measured_j = None)."}
>
  <section class="px-6 md:px-10 pt-16 md:pt-24 pb-20 max-w-3xl mx-auto">
    <p class="section-label mb-4">Research paper · about</p>
    <h1 class="text-3xl md:text-5xl font-bold text-brand-white tracking-tight">
      The Reality Code Engine: One Lossless Form for Native, Verified, Low-Energy Programming
    </h1>
    <p class="mt-6 font-body text-base text-brand-slate leading-relaxed">
      This paper describes the Reality Code Engine (RCE), a system in which a program is written once as a state machine, called a Form, and code in other languages is generated from it. It presents the written specification of the language, the reference checker that implements it and emits Rust, Python, JavaScript and Scala, the Lean proofs of its theorems, registered measurements of the dimension of its programs, and the design of the engine that finds, checks and reuses solutions. It states how the claims could be shown false and the protocols of the measurements still to be made.
    </p>
    <div class="mt-8 glass-card p-6">
      <p class="font-mono text-[10px] tracking-[2px] uppercase text-brand-gray mb-3">In plain words</p>
      <ol class="list-decimal pl-5 space-y-2 font-body text-sm text-brand-slate leading-relaxed">
        <li>A program is written once, in one precise form, and the computer produces the same program in many languages from it.</li>
        <li>Everything a program can do is declared in its form, and the language requires every input to have a declared outcome, a step or a recorded kept step that leaves the state as it was, rather than an undefined one that is found and fixed later.</li>
        <li>When a fix passes the gate, it can be crystallized by structural identity and replayed at microsecond latency so that the same problem does not cost model time or energy twice.</li>
      </ol>
    </div>

    <div class="mt-8 glass-card p-6">
      <p class="font-mono text-[10px] tracking-[2px] uppercase text-brand-gray mb-3">Why it is built this way</p>
      <ol class="list-decimal pl-5 space-y-2 font-body text-sm text-brand-slate leading-relaxed">
        <li>Refuse to admit. A program that does not yet have the Form's shape is not run; the checker names the place and the rule. This is the same bargain an ownership checker makes in a systems language: more is checked before the program runs, so less is paid for in production.</li>
        <li>Checks in layers. The grammar, the types, ownership, the <code>requires</code> rules, the examples, the property tests and the mutation scores each catch a different kind of fault, and each is run on every commit.</li>
        <li>Proposers under one gate. A person, a search over existing Forms or a generative system can propose a program or a fix. Only the checker decides whether it is admitted, with the same result on every machine, so generated volume does not turn into unchecked complexity.</li>
        <li>Joules as the unit of cost. Pricing a correct program in joules is the OpenIE extension of this argument: the checker's internal cost units are deterministic integers, while first operating-system/on-chip <code>reported_j</code> receipts on Apple M5 Max record <code>49.04 mJ</code> per Form check and <code>15.44 J</code> per full 62-Form suite (<code>measured_j = None</code>, <code>board_metered = false</code>).</li>
      </ol>
      <p class="mt-3 font-body text-xs text-brand-gray leading-relaxed">These points converge with arguments made in public by others in systems programming, for example on ownership and on layered validation; that is an observation about the ideas, not an endorsement of OpenIE by anyone.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="mt-8 glass-card p-6">
      <p class="font-mono text-[10px] tracking-[2px] uppercase text-brand-gray mb-3">Where it stands (22 November 2026, at commit 1762fb4b)</p>
      <ul class="list-disc pl-5 space-y-2 font-body text-sm text-brand-slate leading-relaxed">
        <li>At public commit <code>1762fb4b</code> (20 November 2026), the Lean 4 audit verifies <code>5,537</code> theorem constants in the kernel environment across <code>64</code> top-level <code>RCE</code> modules (<code>1,264</code> theorem and lemma declarations in source, theorems <code>T1</code> to <code>T63</code>, standard axioms only, zero <code>sorry</code>), alongside <code>837</code> Rust checker unit tests across <code>159</code> test files and <code>53</code> built-in standard-library modules.</li>
        <li>All 311 normative clauses of the language have at least one conformance test. Of the <code>2,236</code> conformance tests, the reference interpreter passes <code>2,221</code> with <code>0</code> failures and <code>15</code> unsupported; <code>rce-spec-check</code> accepts <code>62/62</code> valid example Forms and flags <code>13/13</code> syntax and <code>73/73</code> type/admissibility negative examples as requiring change (zero grammar conflicts across <code>118</code> rules and <code>1,206</code> states). The Python and JavaScript emitters each pass <code>1,462</code> of the tests that apply to them, with <code>0</code> failures.</li>
        <li>First on-chip <code>reported_j</code> receipts recorded on Apple M5 Max (<code>macOS 27.2.1</code>, per-process kernel <code>proc_pid_rusage(RUSAGE_INFO_V6).ri_energy_nj</code> <code>L3</code> counter paired with <code>macmon</code> <code>IOReport</code> <code>L2</code>): <code>49.04 mJ</code> median per Form check (<code>7.32 ms</code>/Form, <code>3.04 J</code> per 62-Form pass across 20 passes) and <code>15.44 J</code> per full 62-Form check, trace-run and five-target emit lifecycle (<code>2.32 s</code>/suite). Across the 151-task <code>rce repair</code> ladder, <code>rce repair criterion</code> verifies the 2% stochastic promotion ceiling on external proposals (<code>116/151</code> exact closes, <code>3/119 = 2.5% &gt; 2.0%</code> observed rung-<code>f</code> closes, <code>3/3</code> passing <code>W-MUT-1</code>, <code>0</code> contradictions, keeping rung <code>f</code> in <code>observe</code> mode).</li>
        <li>Not yet: there is no production optimizing compiler, and external inline wall-plug or shunt-rail power probes remain uncollected (<code>measured_j = None</code>, <code>board_metered = false</code>; the KV260 (AMD Kria) board rows filed as <code>measured_j</code> are in Metered Commit Gates, not here). Specification draft 0.9 remains under review.</li>
      </ul>
      <p class="mt-3 font-body text-xs text-brand-gray leading-relaxed">Everything above can be rerun from <a href="https://github.com/openIE-dev/rce">openIE-dev/rce</a> at commit <code>1762fb4b</code>: the conformance suite is in <code>conformance/</code>, the Lean audit is <code>scripts/lean-audit.sh</code>, and the counts are recorded in <code>src/data/rce-facts.json</code>.</p>
    </div>


